/// What the Clip tab's Timing section says about one clip's reverse, and the
/// state machine behind the "Render Reverse" button.
///
/// There is no state that means "reversed and playing forwards", which is the
/// point: every state below either has the frames or says out loud that it
/// does not.
enum ReverseStatus: Equatable {
    /// Not reversed — or reversed media that needs no rendering, which a
    /// still picture is.
    case off
    /// Reversed, with the backwards copy on disk.
    case ready
    /// Reversed, and its frames are being made or about to be.
    case pending
    /// Reversed, and the render was cancelled or failed. Nothing will retry it
    /// on its own; the button does.
    case stopped
    /// Reversed, but the media itself is missing or has no length — the clip
    /// is already drawing a missing-media slate for a different reason.
    case unavailable

    /// The row's own words.
    var summary: String {
        switch self {
        case .off: return "—"
        case .ready: return "Rendered"
        case .pending: return "Rendering…"
        case .stopped: return "Not rendered"
        case .unavailable: return "Media missing"
        }
    }
}

/// The process-wide place `CompositionBuilder` finds a document's reverse
/// resolution, keyed by the same `RecipeSource` slot the compositor reads
/// recipes under — `AudioDSPSource`'s shape, for `AudioDSPSource`'s reasons.
///
/// The two mirrors exist for the same underlying fact: `CompositionBuilder` is
/// handed the composition PLAN, and the plan is `plan.rs`'s structural
import AVFoundation
import AppKit
import VideoToolbox

// MARK: - The renderer

/// Decodes one media file and writes it back to front, so AVFoundation can
/// play a reversed clip forward (spec §11: "reverse is not native; M3
/// pre-renders reversed segments").
///
/// **The reader-to-writer shape is `ExporterWriter`'s**, and deliberately so:
/// an `AVAssetReader` feeding an `AVAssetWriter`, cancelled by a flag every
/// sample checks, progress kept by hand because `AVAssetWriter` has none. What
/// differs is the ONE thing reversal forces — a writer's inputs take samples
/// in ascending presentation order, and the frames arrive in the opposite one,
/// so the file cannot be streamed through. Frames have to be held.
///
/// **Chunks, because holding the whole film is not an option.** The asset is
/// walked backwards a slice at a time: read `[t, t + Δ)` into memory, append
/// its frames newest-first, then step back to `[t − Δ, t)`. Δ is sized by
/// BYTES rather than by seconds — 4K frames are sixteen times a 720p frame —
/// so the resident cost is bounded whatever the source is, at the price of one
/// `AVAssetReader` per slice.
///
/// **The arithmetic that makes the reversed file line up with the model.**
/// The pivot is the media's own duration `T` as the model recorded it and the
/// subtraction is `ReverseMirror`'s, which states the convention and why the
/// app owns it; the reversal works only because it is exactly as long as the
/// original. Frame durations come out of the gaps between presentation stamps
/// rather than out of a nominal rate, which keeps a variable-rate source exact
/// instead of nearly right, and the written stream tiles `[0, T)` with no seam.
///
/// **What it does NOT do.**
/// * It does not carry the source's `preferredTransform`, and must not. A
///   custom compositor gets RAW decoded frames and turns the sampling itself
///   from the orientation code `CompositionBuilder.orientationCodes` reads off
///   the FORWARD asset (§7.5). The frames written here are the same coded
///   frames the forward file decodes to, so that one code is right for both;
///   baking a transform in would turn a portrait clip twice.
/// * It does not preserve an alpha channel. The intermediate (spec §11 PR-7)
///   is 10-bit 4:2:0 HEVC Main 10, or 10-bit 4:2:2 ProRes 422 when the source
///   has an odd width or height, so a reversed ProRes 4444 clip loses its
///   transparency — the price of an intermediate small enough to live in the
///   package, and not silent: the picture changes visibly when the clip is
///   reversed.
/// * It does not convert colour. The frames are read in the source's own
///   YCbCr at 10 bits, the held copy keeps the decoder's colour attachments,
///   and the encoder is given no colour properties, so the file carries the
///   source's own primaries, transfer and matrix — an HLG clip's reverse is
///   labelled HLG — and the compositor treats it exactly as it treats the
///   forward file (GR-15: HDR is converted there, at step 0, not here).
final class ReverseRenderer {

    /// What one reverse is written as (spec §11 PR-7), from the source's
    /// coded size. HEVC 4:2:0 cannot hold an odd size — 1919×1079 came back
    /// 1918×1078 — and `videoSettings` says why the size must be exact, so an
    /// odd width or height takes ProRes 422, whose file is several times
    /// larger, for its exact size.
    enum Intermediate: Equatable {
        /// Even width and height: `x420` frames, HEVC Main 10.
        case hevcMain10
        /// An odd width or height: `x422` frames, ProRes 422 — full-range
        /// frames when the source is flagged full range (PR-10).
        case proRes422

        init(_ dimensions: CMVideoDimensions) {
            self = dimensions.width % 2 != 0 || dimensions.height % 2 != 0 ? .proRes422 : .hevcMain10
        }

        /// The format the reader is asked for, held, and handed the writer.
        var pixelFormat: OSType {
            switch self {
            case .hevcMain10: return REDACTED
            case .proRes422: return REDACTED
            }
        }

        /// The format for a source whose forward video track is flagged
        /// `fullRange` (GR-22's flag, spec §11 PR-10): ProRes 422 reads
        /// full-range `x422`, because a full-range file read as video-range
        /// `x422` came back up to 90 codes off (log_proof L13, #289). The HEVC
        /// branch reads `pixelFormat` whatever the flag.
        func pixelFormat(fullRange: Bool) -> OSType {
            if fullRange && self == .proRes422 {
                return REDACTED
            }
            return pixelFormat
        }

        /// Bytes one held pixel costs: 10-bit samples in 16-bit words, one
        /// luma and half (4:2:0) or one (4:2:2) chroma sample a pixel.
        var bytesPerPixel: Int {
            switch self {
            case .hevcMain10: return 3
            case .proRes422: return 4
            }
        }
    }

    /// One media file to reverse, decided on the main thread against the
    /// document's snapshot so the render loop consults nothing that can move.
    struct Job: Equatable {
        let media: UInt32
        /// The media's display name, for the sheet's status line.
        let name: String
        let source: URL
        let destination: URL
        /// The media's duration as the MODEL has it — the pivot the reversal
        /// is measured about, and the number `in` is measured against.
        let duration: Ticks
        let fps: Fps
        let hasAudio: Bool
    }

    enum ReverseError: LocalizedError {
        /// Nothing in the file can be reversed: no readable track, or a
        /// duration the model does not believe in.
        case unreadable(String)
        case cancelled
        case failed(String)
import AVFoundation
import VideoToolbox

extension ReverseRenderer {
    // MARK: - Settings

    /// The coded frame size, read from one decoded frame at the head of the
    /// file. Nil means "no picture", which an audio-only source really has and
    /// an unreadable video track amounts to.
    static func dimensions(
        of asset: AVURLAsset, track: AVAssetTrack, limit: CMTime
    ) -> CMVideoDimensions? {
        guard let reader = try? AVAssetReader(asset: asset) else { return nil }
        reader.timeRange = CMTimeRange(
            start: .zero,
            duration: CMTimeMinimum(limit, CompositionBuilder.time(ReelTime.perSecond)))
        let output = AVAssetReaderTrackOutput(
            track: track,
            outputSettings: [
                kCVPixelBufferPixelFormatTypeKey as String: Int(REDACTED)
            ])
        guard reader.canAdd(output) else { return nil }
        reader.add(output)
        guard reader.startReading() else { return nil }
        defer { reader.cancelReading() }
        guard let sample = output.copyNextSampleBuffer(),
            let format = CMSampleBufferGetFormatDescription(sample)
        else { return nil }
        return CMVideoFormatDescriptionGetDimensions(format)
    }

    /// The encoder settings for the picture.
    ///
    /// **The size is the source's, exactly.** Not rounded to even, not scaled:
    /// the compositor maps the model's `source_size` across whatever texture
    /// arrives, so a reversed asset that is one pixel narrower than the
    /// forward one would stretch the clip the moment it is reversed.
    ///
    /// **10 bits at every size (spec §11 PR-7).** HEVC pinned to Main 10 —
    /// `ExporterWriter`'s `hevc_10bit` pin — fed the `x420` frames the reader
    /// decoded, at HEVC's rate: generous because this is an intermediate, its
    /// pixels encoded again on the way to the export, where a stingy one is a
    /// generation of loss the user did not ask for. An odd size is ProRes 422
    /// (`Intermediate`), which takes no compression dictionary.
    ///
    /// **No colour properties.** Given none, the writer labels the file by the
    /// frames' own attachments, which `copyBuffer` carries, so an HLG or a
    /// BT.2020 source is labelled as itself. Forcing BT.709 here mislabelled
    /// those sources, and with the attachments kept it would convert them.
    static func videoSettings(
        _ dimensions: CMVideoDimensions, fps: Fps, intermediate: Intermediate
    ) -> [String: Any] {
        let width = Int(dimensions.width)
        let height = Int(dimensions.height)
        guard intermediate == .hevcMain10 else {
            return [
                AVVideoCodecKey: AVVideoCodecType.proRes422,
                AVVideoWidthKey: width,
                AVVideoHeightKey: height,
            ]
        }
        let rate = fps.double > 0 ? fps.double : 30
        let bits = Int(
            min(max(Double(max(1, width * height)) * rate * 0.12, 2_000_000), 240_000_000))
        let compression: [String: Any] = [
            AVVideoAverageBitRateKey: bits,
            // A keyframe at least every two seconds, for the same reason the
            // export sets one: this file is scrubbed and seeked into on every
            // preview frame.
            AVVideoMaxKeyFrameIntervalDurationKey: 2.0,
            AVVideoExpectedSourceFrameRateKey: Int(rate.rounded()),
            AVVideoProfileLevelKey: kVTProfileLevel_HEVC_Main10_AutoLevel as String,
        ]
        return [
            AVVideoCodecKey: AVVideoCodecType.hevc,
            AVVideoWidthKey: width,
            AVVideoHeightKey: height,
            AVVideoCompressionPropertiesKey: compression,
        ]
    }
    /// tabs to enable. Refuses when the placement breaks the overlap rule;
    /// `Err` for an unknown track or media, or a media with no usable
    /// duration.
    pub fn add_clip(&self, track: u32, media: u32, at: Ticks) -> Result<Outcome, String> {
        self.require_track(track)?;
        let found = self.require_media(media)?.clone();
        // A drag can start left of zero; the timeline has no negative time.
        let at = self.snapped(at).max(0);

        let duration = self.whole_media_length(&found)?;
        let source = match found.kind {
            MediaKind::Image => Source::Image { media },
            MediaKind::Video | MediaKind::Audio => Source::Media {
                media,
                r#in: 0,
                reversed: false,
            },
            MediaKind::Lut => return Err(crate::lut::lut_not_placeable(media)),
        };

        let has_pixels = found.has_video || matches!(found.kind, MediaKind::Image);
        let video = has_pixels.then(|| VideoProps {
            scale: self.fit_scale(f64::from(found.width), f64::from(found.height)),
            ..VideoProps::default()
        });
        let audio = found.has_audio.then(AudioProps::default);
    /// (`model::Source::Media`), so reversing keeps the in point and
    /// therefore shows different frames — the tail of the file instead of
    /// its head. Advancing it to keep the same frames would be a second
    /// convention, and every trim, split and `source_window.rs`'s
    /// `source_length_on_timeline` would need to know which one was in
    /// force.
    ///
    /// A non-media source is an `Err` and not a refusal — the non-media
    /// rule (`edit.rs`, Results). An identity when the clip already plays
    /// that way. Refused on a linked clip (spec §11 AU-94).
    pub fn set_reversed(&self, clip: u32, reversed: bool) -> Result<Outcome, String> {
        let target = self.require_clip(clip)?;
        let Source::Media {
            media,
            r#in,
            reversed: current,
        } = target.source
        else {
            return Err(format!("clip {clip} is not a media clip"));
        };
        if current == reversed {
            return Ok(Outcome::Identity);
        }

        let mut after = target.clone();
        after.source = Source::Media {
            media,
            r#in,
            reversed,
        };
        if let Some(why) = self.linked_rate_refusal(target, &after) {
            return Ok(Outcome::Refused(why));
        }
        let mut next = self.clone();
        if let Some(slot) = next.clip_mut(clip) {
            *slot = after;
        }
        self.finish(next)
    }
}
//! restating them, so a split made by a range delete is the same split ⌘T
//! makes.

use crate::cursor_props::with_base_cursor;
use crate::edit_clip::clip_end;
use crate::model::{Clip, Enabled, Project, Source, VideoProps};
use crate::outcome::Outcome;
use crate::source_window::source_read_by;
use crate::time::{Ticks, TIMESCALE};

/// Default freeze length (spec §6). Two seconds is long enough for a hold
/// to read as deliberate and short enough that the user trims it rather
/// than deletes it.
const DEFAULT_FREEZE_DURATION: Ticks = 2 * TIMESCALE;

/// A half-open span of project time a ripple removes.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
struct Span {
    start: Ticks,
    end: Ticks,
}

/// The spans merged into disjoint ones, ascending.
///
/// Adjacent spans merge as well as overlapping ones: two clips that meet at
/// a cut leave ONE hole, and shifting twice by two lengths is the same as
/// shifting once by their sum only when the boundaries are handled in one
/// piece. Empty spans are dropped — a zero-length hole moves nothing.
fn union(mut spans: Vec<Span>) -> Vec<Span> {
    spans.sort_by_key(|s| (s.start, s.end));
    let mut out: Vec<Span> = Vec::new();
    for span in spans {
        if span.end <= span.start {
            continue;
        }
        match out.last_mut() {
    /// `at`, **insert time** so `duration` of empty timeline opens across
    /// every unlocked track — which is what keeps the rest of the cut in
    /// sync with the hold (spec §11) — and then fill the hole this clip's
    /// track now has with a `still` naming the source time that was under
    /// the playhead. No image is extracted: a still is one source frame
    /// scaled in the composition (spec §11).
    ///
    /// The source time is `in + (at - start) × speed`, clamped inside the
    /// media so the still names a frame that exists. The still carries the
    /// clip's video properties EVALUATED at the cut, not its base ones: the
    /// frozen frame must sit exactly where the frame it froze sat, and an
    /// action may have been moving it. For the same reason the still gets
    /// no actions — the state it froze at is baked into its base props, and
    /// a copied action would animate away from the frame the cut matched.
    ///
    /// `Err` for an unknown clip, a non-media source, an `at` outside the
    /// clip, or a duration under one frame; refused, in the rule's own
    /// sentence, if the still would break the overlap rule.
    pub fn freeze_frame(
        &self,
        clip: u32,
        at: Ticks,
        duration: Option<Ticks>,
    ) -> Result<Outcome, String> {
        let target = self.require_clip(clip)?.clone();
        let Source::Media { media, r#in, .. } = target.source else {
            return Err(format!("clip {clip} is not a media clip"));
        };
        // A clip with no video props draws nothing, so there is no frame to
        // hold — the same "presence of the group is the aspect" rule the
        // property patches use.
        if target.video.is_none() {
            return Err(format!("clip {clip} has no video properties"));
        }

        let at = self.snapped(at);
        if at < target.start || at > clip_end(&target) {
            return Err(format!("clip {clip} does not cover {at}"));
        }
        let frame = self.frame();
        let duration = match duration {
            None => DEFAULT_FREEZE_DURATION,
            Some(given) => {
                let snapped = self.snapped(given);
                if snapped < frame {
                    return Err(format!(
                        "freeze duration must be at least one frame ({frame} ticks), not {given}"
                    ));
                }
                snapped
            }
        };

        let clip_t = at.saturating_sub(target.start);
        // The source the clip has read by the cut: through its map on a
        // ramped clip, the tick a split's two halves meet at (spec §11
        // SR-18 (f)).
        let source_at = r#in.saturating_add(source_read_by(&target, clip_t)).max(0);
        // The clamp only has to keep the still's source time inside the
        // media, which is `source_window.rs`'s `last_still_time`; a media
        // the project does not have cannot bound anything.
        let limit = self
            .media(media)
            .map_or(source_at, |m| self.last_still_time(m));
        let source_at = source_at.clamp(0, limit);

        let video = with_base_cursor(
            self.evaluate_clip(&target, clip_t).video,
            target.video.as_ref(),
        );
        let mut next = match self.split_clips(&[clip], at)? {
            Outcome::Applied(split) => split,
            // The playhead sat on a clip boundary: nothing to cut, and the
            // freeze still has somewhere to go.
            Outcome::Identity => self.clone(),
            Outcome::Refused(why) => return Ok(Outcome::Refused(why)),
        };
        match next.insert_time(at, duration)? {
            Outcome::Applied(inserted) => next = inserted,
            Outcome::Identity => {}
            Outcome::Refused(why) => return Ok(Outcome::Refused(why)),
        }

        let still = Clip {
            // A held frame has no sound, so the audio side is off and there
            // are no audio props to carry.
            enabled: Enabled {
                video: true,
                audio: false,
            },
            // A still is a NEW clip with new ends, so it does not inherit
            // the preset anchored to the ones it was taken from (format
            // version 8): a freeze taken mid-arrival would otherwise arrive
            // again, and one taken anywhere at all would carry a departure
            // onto a frame that is meant to hold. The evaluated placement
            // it keeps is the un-moved one — `evaluate_clip` never applies
            // motion — so the still sits exactly where the inspector said
            // the clip was, which is the documented jump (road map 5 §5h).
            video: Some(VideoProps {
                motion: None,
                ..video
            }),
            ..Clip::placed(
                next.allocate_id(),
                target.track,
                at,
                duration,
                Source::Still {
                    media,
                    at: source_at,
                },
            )
        };
        if let Err(why) = next.check_overlap_rule(std::slice::from_ref(&still), still.track, &[]) {
            return Ok(Outcome::Refused(why));
        }
        next.clips.push(still);
        self.finish(next)
    }
}

